<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Quedlinburg</h1><p class="st-meta">936 CE – 1803 CE · capital Quedlinburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A convent founded at the tomb of King Henry the Fowler, whose princess-abbesses, often royal women, ruled Quedlinburg for nearly nine centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> died in 936 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>, his widow Matilda and their son Otto I founded a community of canonesses there to pray for him. Otto's daughter Matilda became its first abbess in 966, and while her nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto III</a> was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 997 she acted as regent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> was a favoured Ottonian palace where emperors celebrated Easter, and its abbesses were princesses of the Empire ruling the town and surrounding lands. The treasure includes Ottonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and reliquaries, some stolen in 1945 and recovered in 1993. Abbess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-ii-abbess-of-quedlinburg">Anna II</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1539, and later abbesses were Lutheran princesses, often of the Swedish or Prussian royal houses.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey was secularised in 1803 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Its last abbess was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-albertina-of-sweden">Sophia Albertina of Sweden</a>. The collegiate church and castle hill, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry's</a> tomb, are part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> World Heritage Site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 936 CE – 1803 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.33%;width:17.25%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>936 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quedlinburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Matilda of Quedlinburg, Anna II of Stolberg, Sophia Albertina of Sweden</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Quedlinburg Abbey, Stift Quedlinburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1370&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-quedlinburg" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Quedlinburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 387 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Quedlinburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5251 643L5229 694M5251 643L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5251 643h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5251 643h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1370&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-quedlinburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
